          "text": "Base directory for this skill: /home/leon0399/.claude/skills/creative-thinking-patterns\n\n# Creative Thinking Patterns\n\nA small operational pattern language for active idea generation, perspective-taking, and idea evaluation. Source: Christian Kohls, *Patterns for Creative Thinking* (PLoP 2012).\n\nThe job of this skill is not to *explain* creative methods to the user — it is to **use them**. When a user comes in stuck, vague, or asking for ideas, pick the right pattern (or chain of patterns), apply it concretely, and deliver many options.\n\n## Why patterns, not free-form brainstorm\n\nFree-form \"let's brainstorm\" almost always converges on whatever the user is already thinking. The mind follows its strongest existing path; without a deliberate disruption, ideas stay close to the prompt. The patterns below each work by injecting a specific kind of disruption — a question template, a forced viewpoint switch, an unrelated stimulus. Choose the disruption that fits the stage the user is at.\n\n## Pattern selector\n\nMatch the user's situation to a pattern. Most non-trivial creative requests benefit from chaining 2–3 of these.\n\n| User situation | Start with | Then often |\n|---|---|---|\n| Has an existing idea/product/process to improve | **Idea Triggers** (SCAMPER on the existing thing) | Multiple Perspectives, then evaluate |\n| Has a problem but no ideas yet | **Multiple Perspectives** to scope it, then Idea Triggers to generate | Random Impulse if output feels predictable |\n| Has many ideas already, all in the same direction | **Random Impulse** to break the pattern | Idea Triggers to extend the new direction |\n| Has 1–3 candidate ideas to choose between | **Multiple Perspectives** (Six Hats / stakeholders) on each | Comparison/voting from `pattern-catalog.md` |\n| Vague problem statement | First **Understand the situation** patterns from `references/pattern-catalog.md` (5W1H, rephrase goal) | then go generative |\n| Needs to commit and execute | Skip generation; use **Implement** patterns from `references/pattern-catalog.md` | — |\n\n## The three core patterns (operational summaries)\n\nFull method lists, question templates, and worked examples for each pattern live in the `references/` directory. Read the relevant reference file when you actually apply the pattern — the summaries below tell you what each one does and when, but the references contain the full ammunition (SCAMPER expansions, all six hat question lists, random-stimulus mechanics).\n\n### 1. Idea Triggers\n\n**What it is**: A checklist of structured questions/prompts that push thought in a specific direction. SCAMPER and Osborn's checklist are the classic instances.\n\n**When to use**: The user has an existing thing (product, feature, process, name, design) and wants variations or improvements on it. Best when there's something concrete to mutate.\n\n**How to apply**:\n1. Identify the \"thing\" being modified.\n2. Walk through trigger categories one by one — don't stop at the first idea per category. Aim for 2–3 ideas per trigger.\n3. Suspend judgment during generation (the user will judge later; killing ideas mid-flow stops the flow).\n4. Group results by trigger so the user sees the angle each idea came from.\n\n**Quick inline trigger set** (use this if you don't need the full reference): Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Magnify, Put to other uses, Eliminate, Rearrange/Reverse. Plus: Negate the assumption, Exaggerate, Use a metaphor, Apply effects of time.\n\n**Full reference**: `references/idea-triggers.md` — SCAMPER expanded, Osborn's full checklist, the meta-pattern triggers (Provocation, Substitution, etc.), and the extended question list (negation, what-if, parody, etc.).\n\n### 2. Multiple Perspectives\n\n**What it is**: Deliberately rotate through fixed viewpoints on the same thing, treating each viewpoint as equally valid. The classic instance is De Bono's Six Thinking Hats.\n\n**When to use**: The user is evaluating an idea, scoping a problem with several stakeholders, has tunnel vision on one angle, or needs both critical and generative views without the two killing each other.\n\n**How to apply**:\n1. Pick a perspective set that fits the situation — Six Hats for evaluation, Disney (dreamer/realist/critic) for ideation-to-execution, stakeholder roles for design, \"what would X do?\" for stuck creative work.\n2. Hold each perspective for a full pass before switching. Mixing perspectives mid-thought collapses back to default thinking.\n3. Equal effort on each — do not let the user (or yourself) phone in the perspectives that feel less natural. Those are usually the ones that produce the most useful insight.\n4. Surface contradictions explicitly rather than averaging them away. Contradictions between perspectives are signal, not noise.\n\n**Default perspective set** if nothing else fits: White (facts), Yellow (upside), Black (downside), Green (alternatives). Skip Red/Blue unless they fit.\n\n**Full reference**: `references/multiple-perspectives.md` — full Six Hats question lists, Disney method walkthrough, role/character technique, stakeholder mapping.\n\n### 3. Random Impulse\n\n**What it is**: Inject an unrelated stimulus (random word, image, walk-by observation) and force a connection to the user's problem. The mind, faced with two unrelated items held in attention, will manufacture a bridge — and that bridge is often the new idea.\n\n**When to use**: The user is stuck inside their own framework. Symptoms: their ideas all feel similar, they keep rejecting their own suggestions for the same reason, they explicitly say \"I keep going in circles,\" or you've already used Idea Triggers and the output is still predictable.\n\n**How to apply**:\n1. Generate a random impulse — for text-only contexts, pick a concrete noun (use a varied vocabulary; \"watermelon,\" \"lighthouse,\" \"stapler,\" \"subway,\" \"moss,\" \"auctioneer\"). For richer contexts, draw an image or describe a scene.\n2. List the properties/associations of the impulse openly (don't filter for relevance yet).\n3. For each property, force a bridge to the user's problem using one of: combination, analogy, new context, new way of looking.\n4. Most bridges will be junk. That's expected. The skill is in generating many bridges fast and surfacing the 1–2 surprising ones.\n5. If the first impulse yields nothing, do **not** keep mining it — discard and pick a new one.\n\n**Full reference**: `references/random-impulse.md` — impulse generation methods, the four-bridge framework (combine / analogize / re-context / re-view), worked examples.\n\n## How to chain patterns\n\nReal creative work rarely uses one pattern. Common productive chains:\n\n- **Stuck → Random Impulse → Idea Triggers**: Use the random word to break the rut, then run SCAMPER on the new direction to expand it.\n- **Vague problem → 5W1H (catalog) → Multiple Perspectives → Idea Triggers**: Scope the problem, see it from several angles, then generate against the framing that fits best.\n- **Many ideas → Multiple Perspectives (Six Hats) → Comparison table (catalog)**: Evaluate each candidate from each hat, then aggregate.\n- **One promising idea → Idea Triggers (variation/substitution) → Black hat from Multiple Perspectives**: Generate variants, then stress-test the best ones.\n\nWhen chaining, narrate the transition briefly so the user can follow (\"OK, we have eight options now — let me put on the Black hat to find the failure modes\").\n\n## Operating principles (the \"why\")\n\nThese principles cut across all the patterns. Internalise them rather than reciting them.\n\n1. **Suspend judgment during generation.** Evaluation kills generation. Even a quiet \"hmm, that won't work\" mid-list reduces the number of ideas the next step produces. Generate first, judge later, in a clearly separate phase.\n\n2. **Set an idea quota.** \"Give me three ideas\" produces three ideas. \"Give me twelve\" produces twelve, and the last six are usually better than the first six because the obvious ones are out of the way. When generating, aim for **at least 8–10 candidates** before stopping.\n\n3. **Disrupt to redirect.** If the user follows a path, the next step is most likely to be along the same path. Every pattern in this skill is a deliberate disruption of that path. Don't soften the disruptions — a random impulse that \"kind of relates\" to the topic isn't random and won't work.\n\n4. **Many perspectives can coexist.** When using Multiple Perspectives, contradictions between hats/roles are not problems to resolve. They are the output. Surface them; don't average them.\n\n5. **Generate beats evaluate, until it doesn't.** When the user is in generation mode and receives an idea they like, do **not** immediately stop and refine. Note the keeper, finish the generation pass, then return.\n\n## Reference files\n\nRead these only when you reach the relevant pattern, not upfront:\n\n- `references/idea-triggers.md` — SCAMPER, Osborn, full trigger taxonomy, helper questions per trigger.\n- `references/multiple-perspectives.md` — Six Thinking Hats (full question lists), Disney method, role-play, stakeholder mapping.\n- `references/random-impulse.md` — impulse generation, the four-bridge framework, worked examples.\n- `references/pattern-catalog.md` — the broader Kohls catalog: understand-the-problem patterns (5W1H, fishbone, mind maps, SWOT, force field), evaluation patterns (PMI, value-feasibility matrix, ranking), implementation patterns (prototyping, milestones, sell-your-ideas), and supportive practices (idea logbook, dream team, prepared mind).\n\n## Worked example (compressed)\n\n> **User**: \"I need to come up with a name for a feature in my loyalty SaaS that lets merchants send time-limited bonus rewards. I've been calling it 'Bonus Rewards' which is boring.\"\n\nA good response using this skill:\n\n1. **Recognise**: existing concept (the feature) → start with Idea Triggers, fall back to Random Impulse if output is