          "text": "Base directory for this skill: /home/leon0399/.claude/skills/octocat\n\n# GitHub Skill\n\nUse the `gh` CLI for all GitHub interactions. Never suggest the web UI when `gh` can do it.\n\n## Core mechanics\n\nThese four behaviours cause *silently wrong answers* rather than errors, which is why they lead:\na truncated list and a mis-parsed query both look like a successful, complete result.\n\n### Discover `--json` fields instead of guessing\n\nRun a command with `--json` and **no field list** — it prints every available field, then pick from\nthat list. Saves a guess-and-retry loop and surfaces fields you didn't know existed:\n\n```bash\ngh pr view --json          # prints the full field list, then exits\ngh issue list --json\n```\n\nAdd `--jq '<expr>'` to filter without a separate `jq` process. `--template` (`-T`) shapes text\noutput, but `-T` collides with a body-template flag on `gh pr create` / `gh issue create` — check\n`--help` before using the short form there.\n\n### List commands truncate silently\n\n`gh pr list`, `gh issue list`, and every `gh search` subcommand default to **30 results** and give\nno indication that more exist. Reading 30 of 200 open PRs and reporting \"that's all of them\" is the\nmost common way this skill's output goes wrong.\n\n```bash\ngh pr list -L 200                      # raise the cap explicitly\ngh api repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls --paginate --jq '.[] | .number'\ngh api graphql --paginate --slurp -f query='...'   # --slurp merges pages into one array\n```\n\n`gh pr list` / `gh issue list` do not expose `totalCount` via `--json`. If you need a true total,\nquery it via `gh api graphql`; otherwise treat `-L` as the ceiling for that call and say so.\n\nNested `--json` arrays are capped too, and this one bites hardest because nothing in the output\nsays so. `gh pr view --json files` returns at most 100 entries. On a 115-file PR:\n\n```bash\ngh pr view 140782 -R kubernetes/kubernetes --json changedFiles,files \\\n  --jq '{declared: .changedFiles, returned: (.files | length)}'\n# => {\"declared\":115,\"returned\":100}\n```\n\nThe defence is that the true count usually sits in a sibling scalar field — so ask for both and\ncompare. `changedFiles` pairs with `files`; `subIssues`, `blockedBy`, and `blocking` are shaped\n`{\"nodes\": [...], \"totalCount\": N}` (capped at 100, 50, and 50) and carry their own `totalCount`.\nIf the numbers disagree, page through `gh api --paginate` instead of trusting the array. Saying\n\"this PR touches 100 files\" when it touches 115 is the kind of error nobody catches downstream.\n\n### Target the repo explicitly\n\n`gh` infers the repo from the cwd's git remotes. Pass `--repo OWNER/REPO` (`-R`) whenever you're\nacting on a repo other than the one you're standing in — or whenever you're not certain which that\nis. Do the same for `gh api`: the `{owner}/{repo}` placeholder is auto-filled from detected remotes,\nwhich is convenient interactively and a liability in a script. Write the values literally when the\nanswer needs to be deterministic.\n\n### Don't fight the terminal\n\n`gh` already behaves correctly in non-TTY contexts — it skips the pager, strips color, and fails\nfast with a useful message instead of prompting. There is no `--no-pager` flag and setting\n`GH_PAGER` defensively is unnecessary. If you *want* TTY-style tables and color, set `GH_FORCE_TTY=1`\ndeliberately.\n\n## Reading files from a repo without cloning\n\nTwo calls answer most \"what's in that repo\" questions, and neither needs a working copy. Reach for\nthem before cloning anything — a clone pays for an entire checkout, and the question usually turns\nout to need two or three named files.\n\nStart with structure, so you're choosing paths instead of guessing them. One call returns every path\nwith its blob size:\n\n```bash\ngh api 'repos/OWNER/REPO/git/trees/HEAD?recursive=1' \\\n  --jq '.tree[] | select(.type==\"blob\") | \"\\(.size)\\t\\(.path)\"'\n```\n\nCheck the sibling `truncate